        "source": "claude",
        "summary": "The Strait of Hormuz remains under severe operational stress. The IMF PortWatch published count for 2026-08-08 stands at 8 transits, against a pre-crisis baseline of 73 per day; PortWatch publishes weekly on Tuesdays, so a multi-day lag is expected. Separately, port arrivals across the Hormuz-area ports on both the Persian Gulf and Gulf of Oman sides reached 814 in the most recent 24 hours, a figure that uses a different methodology and should not be equated to the PortWatch transit measure. As of 08:53 UTC, 372 vessels were holding position away from berth, excluding any ship within 25 km of a working port. Brent traded at $89.34 as of 08:53 UTC, up 0.25% over the prior 24 hours; worldoil.com reports that traders are questioning progress toward a Hormuz deal, though the site does not attribute causation independently. On the policy front, Iranian state media reports that Iran's new security chief has conditioned full reopening on the United States meeting Iran's terms; this is a single claim originating with Iranian state media and is not independently corroborated. Meanwhile, gCaptain reports four crew members killed in a Houthi attack in the Bab el-Mandeb strait, with a separate missile strike reported off Pakistan. The site's Crisis Pressure composite sits in the extreme band, easing slightly over 24 hours, while the Escalation Forecast composite is in the elevated band and rising, a divergence that signals near-term uncertainty even as current pressure shows marginal relief.",
